From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: NOVA Profile <nova@example.invalid>
Date: Sat, 22 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] nova: substitute the screen and the WebGL / canvas surfaces

The first patch covers the values a page reads out of the navigator.
This one covers the surfaces that are read on their own.

The screen
---------

``screen.width``, ``screen.height``, ``screen.availWidth``,
``screen.availHeight``, ``screen.availLeft``, ``screen.availTop``,
``screen.colorDepth``, ``screen.pixelDepth`` and
``window.devicePixelRatio`` describe the display the browser runs on.
Every profile that runs on the same machine reports the same display,
so the screen alone is enough to tell a profile from a real device.

The canvas and the WebGL surfaces
---------------------------------

A canvas readback and a WebGL readback are hashed by the page, and the
hash is stable across runs, so two profiles that differ in nothing but
their configuration produce the same canvas hash.  The WebGL identity
strings -- ``UNMASKED_VENDOR_WEBGL`` and ``UNMASKED_RENDERER_WEBGL`` --
are the same story: they name the graphics stack of the host.

The patch therefore substitutes the values of those surfaces and
perturbs the readback buffers with a deterministic noise that is derived
from the profile, so that the hashes are stable for a profile and
different between profiles.

